{"title":"2002 AMC 12P Problems/Problem 9","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_12P_Problems\" title=\"2002 AMC 12P Problems\">2002 AMC 12P #9</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_10P_Problems\" title=\"2002 AMC 10P Problems\">2002 AMC 10P #16</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_12P_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Two walls and the ceiling of a room meet at right angles at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/c/e3ce33ec2a34dc4fddaec3224043f4a72bbe6d0e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" /> A fly is in the air one meter from one wall, eight meters from the other wall, and nine meters from point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. How many meters is the fly from the ceiling?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/8/1084d45f56f9fc6f2e277623b241dae8dfa79b95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A) }\\sqrt{13} \\qquad \\text{(B) }\\sqrt{14} \\qquad \\text{(C) }\\sqrt{15} \\qquad \\text{(D) }4 \\qquad \\text{(E) }\\sqrt{17}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"430\" height=\"23\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_12P_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can use the formula for the diagonal of a rectangular prism, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/3/c530b6bcbf8c709b69e9457ed195c56dbf2efa83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2+c^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"143\" height=\"21\" /> The problem gives us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/4/eb45a51345ea0185bff1149ad522705823d3ba91.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=1, b=8,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"99\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b19fca7ad0bff616f9c662909a7bc30489490a86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d=9.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" /> Solving gives us: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/0/990f99b15c3d96385c1b4d337c3625bb7fe816c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9=\\sqrt{1^2 + 8^2 + c^2} \\implies c^2=9^2-8^2-1^2 \\implies c^2=16  \\implies c=\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 4}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"576\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p><br />\n~Minor edits by Astro2010~\n~Minor edit by MathCrafter314~\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_12P_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_10P\" title=\"2002 AMC 10P\">2002 AMC 10P</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_10P_Problems\" title=\"2002 AMC 10P Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_10P_Answer_Key\" title=\"2002 AMC 10P Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"ext